            <ArticleTitle>Omental infarction: risk factors in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>233-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">A series of 4 cases of omental infarction are analyzed. It is shown that obesity, as well as idiosyncratic anatomy, are factors in the development of this rare clinicopathologic entity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The authors reviewed the preoperative and operative records of all patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All of the cases of omental infarction were seen in obese children. Other than persistent right-sided abdominal pain, there were no other signs or symptoms. None of the patients had an elevated white blood cell count, and none had fever.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Computed tomography scanning is noted to be helpful in making the diagnosis. Laparoscopy is noted to be the procedure of choice, because standard incisions may preclude one from a proper exploration.</AbstractText>
